  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1210 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nEarly morning weakening convection in northwest Kansas was \nassociated with an upper level trough embedded in the northwest \nflow moving into north central Kansas as of noon today. At the \nsame time a cold front was moving south across western Kansas. \nThis cold front was crossing western Kansas more slowly than \nwhat models indicated yesterday. Even with this slower southward\nmovement, this surface boundary is still forecast to be located\nin extreme southwest Kansas by evening. The main impact from \nthis slower southwest movement is that temperatures this \nafternoon will be a little warmer than what was previously \nthought yesterday. Models however still advertise that some\ncooler air will filter into southwest Kansas behind this cold \nfront tonight with the latest ensembles continuing to indicated\nat 50-70% chance for highs on Thursday to be below 90s degrees \nnorth of US-54 .\n\nAs for precipitation chances over the next 24 hours...there \nwill be a slight chance of afternoon/late day thunderstorms to \nbe possible ahead of the upper level trough that will be \ncrossing north central/central Kansas as the cold front moves \nsouth. The better opportunity for thunderstorms will occur \novernight (50 to near 80%) as the next upper level trough, \nembedded in the ongoing northwest flow crosses southwest Kansas \ngiven the moisture upslope flow and low level forcing that will \nbe present just north of the cold front west of highway 283. A \nfew of these storms overnight will be capable of producing wind \ngusts of 50 to near 60 mph and locally heavy rainfall. \n\nFor Friday through the start of the weekend the 500mb high over\nthe southwest Unites States will shift eastward toward the \nSouthern Plains. This will will result in backing winds which \nwill improve the western downslope flow as precipitation chances\ndecrease. Ensembles remain in good agree with previous runs \nthat by late Saturday as the downslope flow improves then the \n850mb mean temperatures will warm into the upper 20s to lower \n30s. This will be in excess of the 95th percentile of \nclimatology for this time of year. These unseasonably hot \ntemperatures will then continue through Monday as the upper high\nmoves across the Central Plains late this weekend into early \nnext week. These conditions will easily support the current NBM \nforecast with highs of near 100 to 103 being possible, though \ntemperature could be a few degrees warm given the recent NBM \nhigh temperature cool bias during the last heat event.",
